The concentration of reactants decrease with time, whereas that for products increase. In order to measure reaction rates in a laboratory we would need to pick up either a reactant or a product in order to track concentration changes with time. Here we will focus on reactants and we will explore how the numerical changes on concentrations can be translates into reaction rates. Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-Initial-velocities} display the changes in concentration for a general reactant. We can see how the changes are more pronounced at early times and slowly become flat a late times. This is because any reaction tends to slow down as time progresses. The instantaneous rate of reaction $r$ is just the slope on the graph at a given time. 

